47:   ```text
48:   FAIL tests/stress-challenger.test.ts > Challenger 1 Stress & Adversarial Test Suite > 1. Multi-PII Payloads & Interleaved Spans > checks substring collision behavior between short names and longer regular words
49:   AssertionError: expected 'Hello [GIVEN_NAME_1], be careful of the [GIVEN_NAME_2]ger in [CITY_1].' not to contain 'ger'
50: 
51:   Expected: "ger"
52:   Received: "Hello [GIVEN_NAME_1], be careful of the [GIVEN_NAME_2]ger in [CITY_1]."
53:    ❯ tests/stress-challenger.test.ts:69:31
54:        67|       // Defect verification: Does the redacted text mutilate regular English words?
55:        68|       // "Danger" should NOT become "[GIVEN_NAME_2]ger"
56:        69|       expect(result.text).not.toContain("ger");
57:        70|       expect(result.text).toContain("Danger");
58:   ```
59: - **Direct Observations of Data Corruption & Algorithmic Defects**:
60:   1. **Substring Mutilation**: In `currentText = currentText.replace(item.original, placeholder)`, JavaScript performs naive substring replacement without checking word boundaries. Common names (`Dan`, `Rob`, `May`, `Art`, `Will`) corrupt dictionary words (`Danger` becomes `[GIVEN_NAME_2]ger`, `problem` becomes `p[GIVEN_NAME_2]lem`, `robot` becomes `[GIVEN_NAME_3]ot`).
61:   2. **Placeholder Number Collision**: `count` starts from `item.placeholder`'s number without knowing what other native entities exist. If native engine already assigned `[GIVEN_NAME_1]` to Alice and `[GIVEN_NAME_2]` to Bob, a repeated occurrence of Alice (`item.placeholder === "[GIVEN_NAME_1]"`) computes `count = 1 + 1 = 2`, assigning `[GIVEN_NAME_2]` to Alice—colliding directly with Bob!
62:   3. **Potential Infinite Loop**: If `item.original` appears within `placeholder` (e.g. category `TEST` producing `[TEST_1]`), `currentText.includes(item.original)` remains true indefinitely inside the `while` loop.
63:   4. **Contradictory Assertion in Test Suite**: In `tests/stress-challenger.test.ts:68-70`:
64:      ```ts
65:      // "Danger" should NOT become "[GIVEN_NAME_2]ger"
66:      expect(result.text).not.toContain("ger");
67:      expect(result.text).toContain("Danger");
68:      ```
69:      Because the English word `"Danger"` literally ends with the substring `"ger"`, any string containing `"Danger"` is mathematically guaranteed to contain `"ger"`. The test assertion `not.toContain("ger")` is contradictory and fails even when `"Danger"` is perfectly preserved intact! The author intended `not.toContain("]ger")` or `not.toMatch(/\[GIVEN_NAME_\d+\]ger/)`.
70: 
